{{Quote|I thought about a biblical epic, like a Ten Commandments and the dawning of the Force. Where did the Force come from? When did we discover it? When did we know how to use it? And a story just started developing.|James Mangold|<ref name="New movies" />}}
An upcoming live-action ''[[Star Wars]]'' '''film''' about the dawn of the [[Jedi]] by [[James Mangold]] is currently planned.<ref name="New movies" />

==Development==
===Origins===
In the early [[1970]]s, ''[[Star Wars]]'' creator [[George Lucas]] once envisioned the [[Star Wars saga|''Star Wars'' saga]] consisting of nine films, the first which would focus on the origins of the [[Jedi Knight]]s and how they were initiated and trained. [[Original trilogy]] producer [[Gary Kurtz]] was briefed about Lucas' plans, but by [[1980]], Lucas changed his mind and decided to wrap up the series with the [[1983]] film {{Film|VI}}, much to Kurtz's dismay as he revealed to [[TheForce.net]] in light of the prequel trilogy's mixed beginning with the [[1999]] film {{Film|I}}.<ref name="TFN Gary Kurtz's Interview">{{WebCite|url=http://www.theforce.net/latestnews/story/gary_kurtz_reveals_original_plans_for_episodes_19_80270.asp|text=Interviews &ndash; Gary Kurtz Reveals Original Plans for Episodes 1-9|work=[[TheForce.net]]|archivedate=20200107091056}}</ref> Published between [[2012]] and [[2014]], the ''[[Star Wars: Dawn of the Jedi]]'' [[Star Wars comic books|comic]] series by [[John Ostrander]] and [[Jan Duursema]] explored the origins of the [[Jedi/Legends|Je'daii]] in the [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity.<ref name="CBR Interview">{{WebCite|url=https://www.cbr.com/nycc-exclusive-ostrander-and-duursema-unveil-star-wars-dawn-of-the-jedi|text=NYCC Exclusive: Ostrander and Duursema Unveil "Star Wars: Dawn of the Jedi"|author=Shaun Manning|date=2011-10-13|work=[[Wikipedia:Comic Book Resources|''Comic Book Resources'']]|archivedate=20200817183352}}</ref>

On [[February 6]], [[2018]], [[Wikipedia:Game of Thrones (TV series)|''Game of Thrones'']] showrunners [[David Benioff]] and [[D.B. Weiss]] were announced by [[StarWars.com]] to produce and write a [[Untitled Star Wars film series|''Star Wars'' film series]],<ref name="Benioff&Weiss">{{SW|url=news/game-of-thrones-creators-david-benioff-and-d-b-weiss-to-write-and-produce-a-new-series-of-star-wars-films|text=''Game of Thrones'' Creators David Benioff and D.B. Weiss to Write and Produce a New Series of ''Star Wars'' Films|nolive=1}}</ref> which reportedly focused on the time period in which the [[Jedi]] came to exist.<ref name="VarietysClaims">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2019/film/features/star-wars-kathleen-kennedy-david-benioff-db-weiss-1203388611|text='Star Wars': Inside 'Game of Thrones' Creators' Exit and the Pressures Facing Lucasfilm (EXCLUSIVE)|author=Brent Lang|date=2019-10-30|work=[[Wikipedia:Variety (magazine)|''Variety'']]|archivedate=20200721105505}}</ref> Benioff and Weiss reluctantly walked away from their project on [[October 28]], [[2019]] due to scheduling conflicts with [[Netflix]] despite their plans for a [[2022]] release after a hiatus following {{Film|IX}}. [[Lucasfilm]] president [[Kathleen Kennedy]] confirmed her interest in waiting for the duo to return at some point.<ref name="DeadlinesExitNews">{{WebCite|url=https://deadline.com/2019/10/star-wars-setback-game-of-thrones-duo-david-benioff-d-b-weiss-exit-trilogy-1202771184|text='Star Wars' Setback: 'Game Of Thrones' Duo David Benioff & D.B. Weiss Exit Trilogy|author=Geoff Boucher|date=2019-10-28|work=[[Wikipedia:Deadline Hollywood|''Deadline Hollywood'']]|archivedate=20200721195000}}</ref> At some point around 2019, [[David S. Goyer]] was signed on to write a film regarding the Jedi's origins 25,000 [[Standard year|years]] before ''The Phantom Menace'', alongside a [[Untitled Jabba the Hutt film|separate project]] under director [[Guillermo del Toro]]. However, Goyer left his work for Lucasfilm and the latter project became shelved.<ref name="DelToroMovie" />

===Pre-production===
{{Quote|Jim Mangold and Beau Willimon wrote an incredible script, but it is definitely breaking the mold and it's on hold.|Kathleen Kennedy in [[2026]]|<ref name="Exit interview">{{WebCite|url=https://deadline.com/2026/01/kathleen-kennedy-exit-interview-1236665253/|text=A Shift In The Force For Lucasfilm’s Kathleen Kennedy: The Exit Interview|author=Mike Flemming Jr.|date=2026-01-15|work=[[Wikipedia:Deadline Hollywood|''Deadline'']]|archivedate=20260115233219}}</ref>}}
After the Disney purchase of Lucasfilm, filmmaker [[James Mangold]] was attached to making a [[Boba Fett]] film in the style of [[Wikipedia:Sergio Leone|Sergio Leone]]. [[Untitled Boba Fett film|This project]] eventually morphed into the series ''[[Star Wars: The Book of Boba Fett]]''.<ref name="">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2023/film/news/indiana-jones-5-james-mangold-harrison-ford-1235650894|text='Indiana Jones 5' Director James Mangold on ‘Pinch-Hitting’ for Spielberg and Refusing Fan Service: Franchises Have Become ‘Large-Scale Advertising’|author=Adam B. Vary|date=2023-06-21|work=[[Wikipedia:Variety (magazine)|''Variety'']]|archivedate=20230622185744}}</ref> Mangold was then hired by Lucasfilm to direct and co-write [[w:c:indianajones:Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny|''Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny'']], the final ''[[Indiana Jones]]'' film. On [[April 7]], [[2023]], it was confirmed during [[Celebration Europe (2023)|Celebration Europe]] that Mangold would direct a ''Star Wars'' film centered on the origins of the Jedi.<ref name="New movies" /> After the panel, Mangold revealed to the [[Wikipedia:Variety (magazine)|''Variety'']] magazine that his film would take place during the dawn of the Jedi and flesh out the exact origins of [[The Force]], like how it was formed and discovered by the [[Prime Jedi|first Jedi]]. Having been drawn at the prospect of making a ''Star Wars'' film in the vein of a [[Wikipedia:Religion|religious]] [[Wikipedia:Epic film|epic]] like [[Wikipedia:Cecil B. DeMille|Cecil B. DeMille's]] [[1950s#1956|1956]] film [[Wikipedia:The Ten Commandments (1956 film)|''The Ten Commandments'']] or [[Wikipedia:William Wyler|William Wyler's]] [[1950s#1959|1959]] film [[Wikipedia:Ben-Hur (1959 film)|''Ben-Hur'']], Mangold discussed these references with Kennedy to convince her of the potential to explore the concept of the Force due to having evolved into a "kind of religious legend" due to encompassing across the ''Star Wars'' saga.<ref name="MangoldsStoryPlans">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2023/film/news/star-wars-movies-dave-filoni-james-mangold-1235576542|text=‘Star Wars' Movies: James Mangold, Dave Filoni to Direct New Films|author=Manori Ravindran|date=2023-04-07|archivedate=20230408005824}}</ref> A [[Wikipedia:The Hollywood Reporter|''The Hollywood Reporter'']] article stated that the title of the film is ''Dawn of the Jedi''.<ref name="THR 3BP">{{WebCite|url=https://https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/3-body-problem-benioff-weiss-netflix-thrones-interview-1235783117|text='Game of Thrones' Creators' Wild Road to Their Biggest Gamble Yet: Netflix's '3 Body Problem'|author=James Hibberd|date=2024-01-10|work=[[Wikipedia:The Hollywood Reporter|''The Hollywood Reporter'']]|archivedate=20240111023931}}</ref>

On [[February 27]], [[2025]], Lucasfilm president [[Kathleen Kennedy]] revealed to [[Wikipedia:Deadline|''Deadline'']] that Mangold was currently working on a script, and was delayed by directing [[Wikipedia:A Complete Unknown|''A Complete Unknown'']] and awards season due to the film's success.<ref name="2025 KK Q&A">{{WebCite|url=https://deadline.com/2025/02/kathleen-kennedy-clarifies-lucasfilm-exit-star-wars-future-1236304421|text=Kathleen Kennedy Speaks On Her Lucasfilm Plans — She Is Not Soon Retiring — & The Films That Will Keep Her In 'Star Wars' Orbit For Years To Come|work=[[Wikipedia:Deadline|''Deadline'']]|archivedate=20250228034942}}</ref> In [[January]] [[2026]], Kennedy commented that Mangold and writer [[Beau Willimon]] had created "an incredible script" that "is definitely breaking the mold," but she said it was on hold, commenting later in the interview that it was "really on the backburner" alongside<ref name="Exit interview" /> the cancelled film<ref name="AP">{{WebCite|url=https://apnews.com/article/adam-driver-star-wars-soderbergh-jarmusch-4e08164d0419759f1b5b50d69864975d|text=Adam Driver on Jarmusch, 'Star Wars' and putting filmmakers first|author=Coyle, Jake|date=2025-10-20|work=[[Wikipedia:Associated Press|Associated Press]]|archivedate=20251020222002}}</ref> ''[[The Hunt for Ben Solo]]'' and [[Donald Glover]]'s [[Lando (film)|''Lando'']] film.<ref name="Exit interview" />

